    title: "Check Engine Light On? Here's What It Actually Means",
    metaDescription: "What triggers a check engine light, when it's an emergency, and why reading a code is not the same as diagnosing the problem.",
    teaser: "Steady vs. flashing, the gas-cap myth that's actually true, and why a $20 code reader can't tell you what's really wrong.",
    bodyHtml: "<p>Few dashboard lights cause as much anxiety as the check engine light. The good news: it's often something minor. The important news: a few situations mean you should act now. Here's how to tell the difference.</p>\n<h2>Steady vs. flashing</h2>\n<p>A <strong>steady</strong> light means the car detected an issue you should get checked soon, but you can usually keep driving carefully. A <strong>flashing</strong> light is serious, it signals an active misfire that can dump raw fuel into and destroy your catalytic converter. If it's flashing, ease off the throttle and get to a shop; don't keep driving hard.</p>\n<h2>The gas-cap thing is real</h2>\n<p>One of the most common (and cheapest) triggers really is a loose, damaged, or missing gas cap, it creates an evaporative-system leak the car detects. Tighten it; if the light doesn't clear after a few drives, it's something else.</p>\n<h2>Why a code isn't a diagnosis</h2>\n<p>A parts-store scanner reads a trouble code like \"P0420,\" but that code points to a <em>symptom</em>, not a definitive part. P0420 might be a catalytic converter, or an upstream oxygen sensor, or an exhaust leak. Replacing the converter on a guess is how people waste hundreds of dollars. Proper <a href=\"/services/engine-diagnostics\">diagnosis</a> tests to find the actual cause.</p>\n<h2>Common causes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Oxygen or air-fuel sensor</li>\n<li>Loose gas cap / EVAP leak</li>\n<li>Misfire (spark plugs, coils)</li>\n<li>Catalytic converter</li>\n<li>Mass airflow sensor or vacuum leak</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And remember: a lit check engine light is an automatic <a href=\"/services/virginia-emissions-inspection\">emissions</a> failure, so it's worth sorting before renewal time. TAB Motors diagnoses it right the first time, call (703) 243-3080.</p>",
