    slug: "car-battery-life-virginia",
    category: "Batteries",
    title: "Why Car Batteries Die Early in Virginia's Weather",
    metaDescription: "Heat, cold, and short trips all shorten car battery life in the DC area. How long batteries really last here and the warning signs to catch first.",
    teaser: "It's not the cold that kills your battery, it's the summer heat setting up the winter failure. Here's the real story.",
    bodyHtml: "<p>Car batteries in the DC area rarely last as long as people expect. Between muggy summers and cold snaps, three to four years is a typical lifespan here, and the failure almost always comes at the worst moment. Here's why, and how to see it coming.</p>\n<h2>Heat does the damage; cold reveals it</h2>\n<p>Most people blame winter, but it's summer heat that actually wears a battery out, high temperatures accelerate the internal chemistry and evaporate the electrolyte. Then the first cold morning comes along, thickening the oil and raising the power needed to crank, and the already-weakened battery finally can't do it. So the battery \"dies in winter,\" but it was really cooked in July.</p>\n<h2>Warning signs</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Slow, labored cranking when you start the car</li>\n<li>Dashboard lights dimming, or electronics acting up</li>\n<li>You've needed a jump-start recently</li>\n<li>The battery is more than three years old</li>\n<li>A battery or charging warning light</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>It might not be the battery</h2>\n<p>A battery that keeps dying can actually be a failing <strong>alternator</strong> not charging it, or a <strong>parasitic drain</strong> pulling power while parked. That's why a proper test checks the whole <a href=\"/services/battery-alternator-starter\">charging system</a>, not just the battery, so you fix the real problem once.</p>\n<h2>Get ahead of it</h2>\n<p>A battery test takes minutes and is free at TAB Motors. If yours is on the older side or cranking slowly, have it checked before it strands you in a parking lot. Call (703) 243-3080.</p>",
