    title: "That Tire-Pressure Light: TPMS, Explained",
    metaDescription: "What the TPMS tire-pressure light means, why it comes on in cold weather, and when it signals a leak or a failing sensor.",
    teaser: "Why the light loves cold mornings, when it means a real leak, and why 'just add air' sometimes isn't enough.",
    bodyHtml: "<p>The little horseshoe-shaped light with an exclamation point is your Tire Pressure Monitoring System (TPMS), and it confuses a lot of drivers. Here's what it's actually telling you.</p>\n<h2>Why it loves cold mornings</h2>\n<p>Air contracts as it cools, so a cold snap can drop your tire pressure enough to trip the light, even with no leak. Tire pressure falls roughly 1 PSI for every 10°F drop, which is why the light so often appears on the first cold morning of the season. Topping the tires back up to the correct pressure usually clears it.</p>\n<h2>When it means a real problem</h2>\n<p>If the light keeps coming back after you've properly inflated the tires, you likely have a <strong>slow leak</strong>, a nail, a bad valve stem, or a wheel that isn't sealing. And if the light <em>flashes</em> for a bit before staying on, that often points to a <strong>failing TPMS sensor</strong> (the sensors have small batteries that eventually die, usually after several years).</p>\n<h2>Why correct pressure matters</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Underinflated tires wear out faster and unevenly</li>\n<li>They hurt fuel economy</li>\n<li>They can overheat at highway speed, a blowout risk</li>\n<li>Correct pressure gives you the best grip and braking</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Don't just ignore it</h2>\n<p>Taping over the light isn't a fix. At TAB Motors we <a href=\"/services/tire-wheel-service\">diagnose the TPMS</a>, find any leak, set your pressures to spec, and service sensors when needed, so the light goes off and stays off. Call (703) 243-3080.</p>",
